## The Problem: A Fragmented Data Processing Worflow

Biologists conducting environmental reviews often need to cross-reference multiple sources of information to determine which special-status species may be present or occur within or near a proposed project area. This process often requires repeated searches across sources, interpolating the information with the desired boundary locations and depending on the judgement of the individual, which can introduce inconsistencies, becoming a time-consuming endeavour for the biologist drafting the report. At Rincon Consulting, Inc., they are looking to revolutionize California Environmental Quality Assurance (CEQA) biological diversity reporting. 

The process involves numerous manual steps like: 
  - entering project information
  - performing searches across different datasets
  - joining and organizing results 
  - working with different data structures
  - preparing information for regulatory reporting
  
This process posed several problems. It involves
  - repetitive data wrangling
  - potential inconsistencies in reporting
  - difficulty reproducing the same workflow
  - time spent preparing data instead of reproducing it

The overarching goal of the tool was not to simply make the process faster, but to create a more consistent and accessible workflow that could be reused across projects.

